[00:02.84]Proposition thirty-nine would add identification requirements each time Californians
[00:07.78]vote.
[00:09.26]Citizenship is already required for state and federal elections.
[00:13.38]The question is how identity and eligibility are checked,
[00:16.86]not whether those elections should allow noncitizens to vote.
[00:21.72]Today, registration uses identifying
[00:24.86]information.
[00:25.82]Most in-person voters sign a roster;
[00:28.58]mail voters sign their ballot envelope, and election officials
[00:32.04]check that signature.
[00:33.90]The proposal would add government-identification checks before ballots are
[00:38.38]counted.
[00:39.36]Imagine a voter preparing a mail ballot at home.
[00:42.54]Under the proposal, that voter would select a government-issued
[00:45.66]identification in their registration,
[00:48.90]then write its last four digits on the ballot
[00:52.04]envelope.
[00:53.22]Officials would compare those digits with the registration record.
[00:56.24]An election worker helps explain the identification process.
[01:01.24]An in-person voter would instead present government-issued identification.
[01:05.94]The state must offer a free voter identification card
[01:09.40]on request.
[01:11.10]The Legislature would still have to clarify details, including
[01:14.66]which forms of identification qualify.
[01:17.96]Officials would make best efforts using government data to
[01:21.40]verify citizenship on voter rolls, publish annual verification percentages,
[01:25.52]and face state compliance audits every other year.
[01:30.22]Those checks are separate from presenting identification at the
[01:33.44]polling place.
[01:34.80]The analyst estimates annual state and local costs from
[01:38.26]tens of millions to the low hundreds of millions
[01:40.94]of dollars.
[01:42.28]Card demand, implementation rules, staffing, and technology choices affect
[01:47.14]the total.
[01:48.38]A free card still costs government money to provide.
[01:51.18]This is a proposed change, not instructions for casting
[01:54.98]your current ballot.
[01:57.28]For voting today, use the official election instructions and
[02:00.80]your county elections office.
[02:03.14]A voter might favor a consistent identification check as
[02:06.60]another verification layer and a way to strengthen confidence.
[02:11.26]The free-card provision addresses the purchase cost of identification.
[02:15.68]Whether the additional checks deliver benefits proportionate to their
[02:19.40]costs is the question to weigh.
[02:21.74]You might ask what happens to eligible voters without
[02:25.00]documents.
[02:26.38]A skeptical voter could worry about administrative mistakes, obtaining
[02:30.34]documents, or navigating new rules.
[02:33.48]A free card does not remove every time or
[02:36.78]paperwork burden.
[02:38.72]Existing checks already operate, so opponents question the extra
[02:41.96]system’s necessity and its effect on access.
[02:44.64]In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 43
[02:48.38]percent said yes, 55 percent no, and 1 percent
[02:52.54]were unsure.
[02:53.94]These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.
[02:57.86]A later September Berkeley survey found 39 percent yes,
[03:02.34]52 percent no, and 9 percent undecided.
[03:05.94]Question wording and methods differ, so comparing polls does
[03:09.22]not by itself establish a trend.
[03:11.82]The Independent Voter Project survey ending October second found
[03:16.46]43 percent yes and 40 percent no.
[03:20.14]Its methods and wording differ again.
[03:22.86]The disagreement is a reason for caution, not a
[03:26.22]reason to declare a winner.
[03:28.14]Yes adds the proposed requirements;
[03:30.78]no retains the current identification framework.
